{"event":"branch","name":"header-accessor-no-linearize","definition":"Search this Linux kernel snapshot for the seed bug class (reading packet header bytes without first guaranteeing they exist in the sk_buff's linear area, as in the phonet pep_get_sb use-after-free) expressed through the skb header accessors instead of raw skb->data casts. Method: grep net/ and drivers/net/ for 'skb_network_header', 'skb_transport_header', 'skb_mac_header', 'skb_inner_network_header', 'skb_inner_transport_header', 'skb_inner_mac_header', and 'skb_reset_network_header'/'skb_reset_transport_header'/'skb_set_network_header'/'skb_set_transport_header'; for every hit inside a receive, decap, or forwarding path (packet_type handlers, *_rcv/*_recv/*_input/*_decode functions, tunnel/encapsulation decapsulation, netfilter hooks, fragment-reassembly output), read the whole enclosing function and check whether the accessor-returned pointer is dereferenced (any ->field read, struct-sized copy, memcmp/memcpy of 2 or more bytes, or of a variable-length header whose length field comes from the packet itself) with no pskb_may_pull()/pskb_may_pull_reason()/skb_linearize() on that path covering exactly those bytes and no skb_headlen()/skb_is_nonlinear()/skb_pagelen() guard. Report only sites reachable by a remote attacker with a crafted non-linear (frag/fraglist/GRO/scatter-gather) skb; record file:line, the verbatim accessor call and dereference, the missing check, and the receive path. Never report net/phonet/pep.c pep_get_sb. Do not use git history, git metadata, or the network.","sources":["f007"]}
{"event":"branch","name":"pull-return-unchecked","definition":"Search this Linux kernel snapshot for a misuse of the skb pull family that stale-pointer searches do not cover: treating a pull as a linearizer or ignoring its failure. Shapes to hunt: (1) a call to pskb_may_pull(), pskb_may_pull_reason(), pskb_pull(), skb_pull(), or __skb_pull() whose NULL/false return is not compared or branched on, followed by code that dereferences skb->data, the pull's would-be result, or a saved header pointer as if the pull had succeeded; (2) skb_pull()/__skb_pull() used as a linearizer: these validate only against skb->len (total bytes, including bytes held in frags/fraglist) and never linearize, so code that checks skb->len >= N, pulls N, and then reads multi-byte or variable-length fields at the new skb->data can read bytes that are not in the linear head — the open template is dn_process_ack() in net/decnet/dn_nsp_in.c around lines 130-158 (reads *ptr after skb_pull(2) with only skb->len checked), which must be verified first and then used as the shape to match elsewhere; (3) pskb_pull() failing after __pskb_pull_tail reallocation while surrounding code proceeds anyway. Method: grep net/ and drivers/net/ for 'skb_pull', 'pskb_pull', '__skb_pull', 'pskb_may_pull', 'pskb_may_pull_reason'; read each enclosing function; flag sites where the return is unchecked before a dependent dereference, or where post-pull reads exceed the bytes that a pull on that path actually guarantees in the linear head. Report only paths a remote attacker reaches with a crafted (possibly non-linear) skb; record file:line with the verbatim length check, pull call, and use lines. Never report net/phonet/pep.c pep_get_sb. Do not use git history, git metadata, or the network.","sources":["f003"]}
{"event":"branch","name":"caller-pointer-across-helper","definition":"Search this Linux kernel snapshot for the seed's stale-pointer-into-skb-head invariant across function boundaries: a function saves a pointer or byte offset derived from an skb's data area (raw skb->data, skb->data + off, a cast of skb->data, an skb_header_pointer() result, an skb_network_header()/skb_transport_header()/skb_mac_header() accessor value, or a pointer/offset stored into an skb->cb control block such as DN_CB, IPCB, or any protocol-private *_skb_cb struct with a ptr/offset field) and then calls a helper, hook, or dispatch callback that can move or free the skb head — pskb_may_pull()/__pskb_pull_tail(), pskb_pull(), pskb_expand_head(), skb_cow()/skb_cow_head(), skb_linearize(), pskb_trim()/skb_carve(), ip_defrag()/fragment reassembly, ip_options_rcv_srr()/ip_forward_options(), netfilter NF_HOOK chains, xfrm transform or outer-mode callbacks, a packet_type/proto .rcv/.input dispatch, or any callee that passes the skb onward — and after the callee returns, the caller (or a later function reading the cb-stored value) dereferences the saved pointer or converts the saved offset against the new skb->data. Method: grep net/ and drivers/net/ for the invalidating helper names above and for pointer-typed or offset-typed fields declared inside per-protocol skb control-block structs; for each helper hit, also read its callers one and two levels up and check every data-derived local or cb field that stays live across the call. Report only cases where the helper can actually reallocate on that path (non-linear or cloned skb) and the saved pointer/offset is used afterwards; record file:line of the caller's save, the helper call, and the use, plus the receive path that lets a remote attacker craft the skb. Never report net/phonet/pep.c pep_get_sb. Do not use git history, git metadata, or the network.","sources":["f003","f004","f006"]}
{"event":"branch","name":"stacks-outside-net-dirs","definition":"Search this Linux kernel snapshot for the seed's skb data-pointer invariant in network protocol code that lives outside net/ and drivers/net/ and is therefore not reached by searches confined to those trees. Target areas: drivers/infiniband (especially ulp/ipoib and core MAD/CMA receive paths), drivers/s390/net (qeth, lcs, ctc), drivers/nfc, drivers/bluetooth (HCI transport packet parsers), drivers/hsi and modem-protocol code under drivers/tty, drivers/isdn, drivers/atm, drivers/staging networking drivers, and any other socket/packet-handling code under drivers/ or elsewhere outside net/ and drivers/net/. In those files hunt all of these: (a) a pointer into the skb linear head (from skb->data, skb->data + off, a cast of skb->data, an skb_header_pointer() return, or skb_network_header()/skb_transport_header() accessors) saved before pskb_may_pull()/pskb_pull() and dereferenced after without refetch; (b) the same staleness across pskb_expand_head(), skb_cow()/skb_cow_head(), skb_linearize(), pskb_trim(), skb_carve(), skb_realloc_headroom(); (c) reads of multi-byte or attacker-length-driven headers at skb->data with no pskb_may_pull/skb_headlen guarantee on a receive path; (d) skb or skb-data pointers used after the skb is freed, handed off via netif_rx()/netif_receive_skb()/skb_queue_tail()/sock_queue_rcv_skb(), or stored into per-device/per-channel/per-firmware private state. Method: grep those directories for pskb_may_pull, pskb_pull, skb_pull, skb_cow, pskb_trim, skb_linearize, skb_header_pointer, kfree_skb and audit each enclosing function plus its callers. Report file:line with the verbatim save, invalidating call, and use lines, and the path by which an attacker-supplied packet reaches the code. Never report net/phonet/pep.c pep_get_sb. Do not use git history, git metadata, or the network.","sources":["f004","f006"]}

